<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Angoulême</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Angoulême is a city in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on a hill above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charente">Charente</a>, capital of the Charente <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>. From 866 it was the seat of counts descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vulgrin-i-count-of-angouleme">Vulgrin I</a>, later the Taillefer line and the Lusignans, and it passed to the French crown in 1308. Its domed Romanesque cathedral of Saint-Pierre dates from the 12th century, and the city now hosts a major comics festival.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/angouleme.jpg" alt="Angoulême" width="320" height="352" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Foulques1996 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Angoulememosaique.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Angoulême stands on a plateau overlooking a meander of the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charente">Charente</a>, and is nicknamed the balcony of the southwest. The old city is built on a rocky outcrop at an altitude of 102 m, above a cliff 80 m high. Ramparts have surrounded the plateau since Roman times and were repeatedly destroyed and rebuilt until reconstruction stopped in the 19th century. The city's position at the crossroads of key routes exposed it to many sieges.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Little is known of the city before the Roman period, except that the plateau held an oppidum called Iculisma. Held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a>, who followed the Arian form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, the city was first besieged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clovis-i">Clovis</a> in 507 and taken in 508, miraculously according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gregory-of-tours">Gregory of Tours</a>. On 18 May 1204 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> signed a charter creating the commune of Angoulême. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-bretigny">Treaty of Brétigny</a> the city passed in 1360 to the English, and John Chandos took possession of it for Edward III in October 1361.</p><p>Angoulême was the seat of the County of Angoumois, which came to a branch of the Valois family from which came François I, born in Cognac in 1494. In 1524 the navigator Giovanni da Verrazzano told François I that he had discovered a new territory, which he named New Angoulême in the king's honour; the area later became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-amsterdam">New Amsterdam</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. In the Revolution the city was called Mountagne-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charente">Charente</a>. German troops arrived on 24 June 1940 and neutralised between 10 and 20 thousand French soldiers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Angoulême is the prefecture of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charente">Charente</a>, with a population a little under 42,000. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papermaking">Papermaking</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> have been connected with the town since the 14th century, helped by the steady temperature and volume of the water of the river Touvre. The cathedral of Saint-Pierre, a Romanesque church of the 12th century, was partly rebuilt in the later 19th century by Paul Abadie. The town hall, also by Abadie, keeps two 13th-century towers, Lusignan and Valois, from the castle of the counts. Since 1983 the city has had a school of fine arts with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s first cartoon section.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Angoulême” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The dukes commanded the service of their vassals, the counts and viscounts of <b>Angoulême</b>, Périgord, La Marche and Limoges and the lords of the castles that multiplied across the region from the late tenth century, but these vassals were often in revolt, and much ducal warfare was spent…</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Duchy of Aquitaine · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>King John of England married Isabella of Angoulême, heiress of the county of <b>Angoulême</b>, in Angoulême Cathedral.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-24">24 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Louis XII died without a son, and his twenty-year-old cousin and son-in-law Francis of <b>Angoulême</b> succeeded him.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-1">1 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Isabella of Angoulême, heiress of the county of <b>Angoulême</b> and second wife of King John, was crowned queen of England.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-8">8 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 866 Charles the Bald appointed his kinsman Vulgrin I count of <b>Angoulême</b> and Périgord to defend the region against Viking raids.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-angouleme">County of Angoulême</a></li><li><blockquote>In 866 Charles the Bald entrusted the counties of Périgord and <b>Angoulême</b> to Vulgrin, a kinsman, to resist Viking raids along the rivers of western France.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-perigord">County of Périgord</a></li></ul></section>
